    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">Io</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">sono</span> Marco. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Abito</span> a Bologna. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Lei</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">lavora</span> qui? · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Voi</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">siete</span> di Torino?",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>tu</strong> is for one person you know well: friends, family, children, classmates, often colleagues. Young people use <em>tu</em> with each other almost always.",
        "tag": "tu"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Lei</strong> is the polite “you”: at the Comune, at the Questura, with a doctor, a landlord or a stranger. The verb is the same as for <em>lui</em> and <em>lei</em>: <em>Lei abita qui?</em> In writing it often has a capital L. If you are not sure, start with <em>Lei</em>.",
        "tag": "Lei"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>lui</strong> = he, <strong>lei</strong> = she. <strong>voi</strong> is “you” for two or more people, friendly or polite. <strong>loro</strong> = they, for men, women or a mixed group.",
        "tag": "lui, lei, voi, loro"
      },
      {
        "html": "The verb ending shows the person, so people usually drop the pronoun: <em>Parlo italiano. Abbiamo due figli.</em> Use the pronoun for contrast or to be clear: <em>Io lavoro e lei studia.</em>",
        "tag": "Drop it"
      },
      {
        "html": "There is no pronoun for “it” as a subject: <em>È grande. Costa dieci euro.</em> Not: <em>Esso è grande.</em> (<em>esso</em> and <em>essa</em> exist only in old or very formal writing.)",
        "tag": "No “it”"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Io sono, io abito, io lavoro</em> in every sentence → ✓ say <em>io</em> once or not at all. ✗ <em>Lei sei…?</em> → ✓ <em>Lei è…?</em> (Lei takes the lui/lei form). ✗ <em>tu</em> with an official → ✓ <em>Lei</em> until they offer <em>tu</em>. Next: <a href=\"/present-regular\">Present tense: regular verbs</a> and <a href=\"/stem-changing-verbs\">Essere and avere</a>.",
    "reference": {
